{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQgGymkwR7HfTmb8E93n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FsUYDmhMj65xgLnB4HqZt1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/skill/references/stdio.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6260,"cache_read_input_tokens":10502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6260},"output_tokens":42,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"accd1a70-1d10-43fa-9d1f-e916b5212e32","uuid":"70ba3ad4-80ec-4512-a29e-e141b61e2e98","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:18:38.476Z","request_id":"req_011CgQgGyTQ9EgoUpbnGiUkp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FsUYDmhMj65xgLnB4HqZt1":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/skill/references/stdio.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FsUYDmhMj65xgLnB4HqZt1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Local process and stdio review\n2\t\n3\tOAuth/HTTP middleware is not the local stdio authorization boundary. Inspect the\n4\thost, process identity, inherited environment, filesystem, network and sandbox.\n5\tPrimary basis: [MCP security guidance](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices).\n6\t\n7\t## Inspect without launching\n8\t\n9\t- Read `.mcp.json`, host settings, executable scripts and container definitions as\n10\t  text. JSON/JSONC/YAML parsing must not source environment files, evaluate shell\n11\t  expressions or invoke constructors. Report redacted secret locations.\n12\t- Trace command, arguments, working directory, environment/PATH and package resolver.\n13\t  Identify shell wrappers, pipe-to-shell installation, unpinned `npx`/`uvx` packages,\n14\t  floating images, executable hooks and generated trusted instruction files.\n15\t  An argv array avoids shell parsing but does not prevent option/path injection.\n16\t- Determine the actual process user, allowed paths/mounts, capabilities, accessible\n17\t  sockets, network policy and credential custody. A local server can inherit the\n18\t  user's full authority even if its tools describe themselves as read-only.\n19\t- Trace symlink/glob/canonical-path handling and every equivalent filesystem/API\n20\t  route. Exact package pins and metadata hashes do not prove implementation safety.\n21\t- Review process-tree termination, child/queued work, open pipes, credential\n22\t  revocation and cleanup. Killing the launching shell does not prove descendants stop.\n23\t- Inspect stdout/stderr and debug logging for protocol contamination and credential\n24\t  exposure, including subprocess stdin and deployment helpers. Do not print secrets\n25\t  discovered in configuration to demonstrate their presence.\n26\t\n27\t## Scanner boundary\n28\t\n29\t[Snyk Agent Scan](https://github.com/snyk/agent-scan) documents that discovery can\n30\texecute configured stdio commands and send configured remote credentials. Treat\n31\tscanner startup as execution of an untrusted supply-chain input. Do not run it as\n32\tpart of a read-only review. An authorized runtime follow-up needs a disposable\n33\tenvironment, fake credentials, bounded egress and reviewed launch commands.\n34\t\n35\tPropose controlled checks for allowed operations plus blocked host/tenant access,\n36\tpath/symlink escapes, unexpected egress, future hook registration and descendants\n37\tsurviving shutdown. Where host configuration is unavailable, identify the owner and\n38\tmissing evidence; a server-only repository cannot establish the host's isolation.\n39\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"accd1a70-1d10-43fa-9d1f-e916b5212e32","uuid":"57b3585a-a1d0-4871-893e-752612c90d86","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:18:38.528Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/skill/references/stdio.md","content":"# Local process and stdio review\n\nOAuth/HTTP middleware is not the local stdio authorization boundary. Inspect the\nhost, process identity, inherited environment, filesystem, network and sandbox.\nPrimary basis: [MCP security guidance](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices).\n\n## Inspect without launching\n\n- Read `.mcp.json`, host settings, executable scripts and container definitions as\n  text. JSON/JSONC/YAML parsing must not source environment files, evaluate shell\n  expressions or invoke constructors. Report redacted secret locations.\n- Trace command, arguments, working directory, environment/PATH and package resolver.\n  Identify shell wrappers, pipe-to-shell installation, unpinned `npx`/`uvx` packages,\n  floating images, executable hooks and generated trusted instruction files.\n  An argv array avoids shell parsing but does not prevent option/path injection.\n- Determine the actual process user, allowed paths/mounts, capabilities, accessible\n  sockets, network policy and credential custody. A local server can inherit the\n  user's full authority even if its tools describe themselves as read-only.\n- Trace symlink/glob/canonical-path handling and every equivalent filesystem/API\n  route. Exact package pins and metadata hashes do not prove implementation safety.\n- Review process-tree termination, child/queued work, open pipes, credential\n  revocation and cleanup. Killing the launching shell does not prove descendants stop.\n- Inspect stdout/stderr and debug logging for protocol contamination and credential\n  exposure, including subprocess stdin and deployment helpers. Do not print secrets\n  discovered in configuration to demonstrate their presence.\n\n## Scanner boundary\n\n[Snyk Agent Scan](https://github.com/snyk/agent-scan) documents that discovery can\nexecute configured stdio commands and send configured remote credentials. Treat\nscanner startup as execution of an untrusted supply-chain input. Do not run it as\npart of a read-only review. An authorized runtime follow-up needs a disposable\nenvironment, fake credentials, bounded egress and reviewed launch commands.\n\nPropose controlled checks for allowed operations plus blocked host/tenant access,\npath/symlink escapes, unexpected egress, future hook registration and descendants\nsurviving shutdown. Where host configuration is unavailable, identify the owner and\nmissing evidence; a server-only repository cannot establish the host's isolation.\n","numLines":39,"startLine":1,"totalLines":39}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FsUYDmhMj65xgLnB4HqZt1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQgGymkwR7HfTmb8E93n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014JGfKD4RqGGDrPTxnWDo9i","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/skill/references/advisories.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6260,"cache_read_input_tokens":10502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6260},"output_tokens":42,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"accd1a70-1d10-43fa-9d1f-e916b5212e32","uuid":"ce0818d3-9169-4778-8125-575a67c52dd5","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:18:38.719Z","request_id":"req_011CgQgGyTQ9EgoUpbnGiUkp","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014JGfKD4RqGGDrPTxnWDo9i":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/skill/references/advisories.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014JGfKD4RqGGDrPTxnWDo9i","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Dated advisory mapping\n2\t\n3\tChecked in BRACE's 2026-10-22 research. This is a starting index, not a live feed,\n4\texhaustive vulnerability database or minimum universally safe version list.\n5\tRecheck the original record when reviewing a matching package. Read affected\n6\tranges and prerequisites before claiming exposure. Record unresolved/opaque remote\n7\tversions as unknown. Branches and prereleases require their own range handling.\n8\t\n9\t## MCP SDKs and server integration\n10\t\n11\t| Original advisory | Issue-specific fix/action to verify | BRACE mapping |\n12\t|---|---|---|\n13\t| [TS shared-server leak GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp), 2026-10-15 | Affected server 2.0.0–<2.3.0: upgrade to 2.3.0 and use fresh-server factories/proper per-request or per-session construction. The advisory excludes stdio and SDK 1.26+. | E06, R04 |\n14\t| [TS validation exhaustion GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453), 2026-10-15 | SDK 1.32.0/server 2.3.0 fix: explicitly configure `maxToolInputElements` (off by default) plus suitable body/parser limits. HTTP applicability matters. | B07, R01, E06 |\n15\t| [TS body/batch exhaustion GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq), 2026-10-15 | Fixes: SDK 1.30.1; server/node 2.1.0; hono 2.0.1. Patched body and batch defaults address different allocations; update each installed affected package. | B07, R01, E06 |\n16\t| [TS issuer binding GHSA-6qxp-vccf-f47h](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6qxp-vccf-f47h), 2026-09-30 | SDK 1.31.0/client/core 2.2.0; configure `expectedIssuer`, bind/clear saved registrations and assess credential rotation/revocation. | B02–B04, E06 |\n17\t| [TS audience GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq), 2026-10-05 | SDK 1.32.0/server 2.3.0/express 2.0.2/server-legacy 2.3.1. Configure `expectedResource` plus verified `AuthInfo.resource`, or enforce audience inside custom verification. Upgrade alone changes nothing. | B02, E06 |\n18\t| [TS redirects GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw), 2026-10-02 | SDK 1.32.0/client 2.3.0. Review custom headers and 307/308 bodies; `redirectPolicy:'follow'` can restore exposure. Ordinary Authorization stripping is a distinct behavior. | B03, B09, E06, E12 |\n19\t| [TS experimental tasks GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw), 2026-10-02 | Affected SDK 1.24.0–<1.32.0: shared taskStore isolation fixed 1.32.0. 2.x excluded. Do not conflate legacy session-based tasks with redesigned Tasks handles. | E06, E09 |\n20\t| [Python issuer binding GHSA-qx49-fqc8-xw99](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-qx49-fqc8-xw99), 2026-09-28 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0; unattended providers require `issuer=`, saved registrations need rebinding/clearing, deprecated provider needs migration. Assess exposure cleanup. | B02–B04, E06, E12 |\n21\t| [Python audience GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23), 2026-10-05 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0 plus `validate_token_resource=True`, correct `resource_server_url`, verified `AccessToken.resource`; custom audience checks may provide an alternative. | B02, E06 |\n22\t| [Python schema refs GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j), 2026-09-28 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0 restricts output-schema resolution. Prior network/file resolution can stall or cause SSRF; advisory does not prove local file bytes reach the server. | R01, B09, E04, E06 |\n23\t| [Python redirects GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j), 2026-10-02 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0; inspect cross-origin custom headers/bodies and any overridden redirect policy. Use final configured endpoints where appropriate. | B03, B09, E06 |\n24\t| [Python body allocation GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc), 2026-09-30 | mcp 1.29.1/2.1.0; check body limits before allocation, including unauthenticated OAuth endpoints and proxy/application parsers. | B07, R01, E06 |\n25\t| [Splunk SVD-2026-1004](https://advisory.splunk.com/advisories/SVD-2026-1004), 2026-10-07 | MCP Server 1.2.1; attacker tool-administrator changes destination, and another authorized executor runs it. Not an unauthenticated universal token leak. | B03, E03, E06, E12 |\n26\t\n27\t## Adjacent components: read only when present\n28\t\n29\t- **Hooks and local executable configuration:** [VS Code GHSA-w79w-rj9h-vg4f](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/security/advisories/GHSA-w79w-rj9h-vg4f),\n30\t  fixed 1.132.1; map B05/B12/C02. A file-write tool may alter future execution.\n31\t- **Filesystem tools and helper fetches:** [LangChain GHSA-gr75-jv2w-4656](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain/security/advisories/GHSA-gr75-jv2w-4656)\n32\t  (glob/symlink/path boundaries) and [GHSA-r7w7-9xr2-qq2r](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain/security/advisories/GHSA-r7w7-9xr2-qq2r)\n33\t  (image-counter DNS rebinding); map B09/B11/R01. Verify package-specific conditions.\n34\t- **Persistent state:** [LangGraph namespace GHSA-6hmw-9432-67qq](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/security/advisories/GHSA-6hmw-9432-67qq),\n35\t  Postgres 3.1.3/SQLite 3.1.2 with checkpoint 4.3.0; [auth-hook GHSA-4hm6-w6qq-w73v](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/security/advisories/GHSA-4hm6-w6qq-w73v),\n36\t  API 0.15.0; [checkpoint revival GHSA-fjqc-hq36-qh5p](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/security/advisories/GHSA-fjqc-hq36-qh5p),\n37\t  checkpoint 4.1.1. Map R04–R06/R14/A04; do not infer safe decoding from a signature.\n38\t- **Model/preprocessing services:** inspect applicable [vLLM advisories](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/security/advisories),\n39\t  including October 20 image dispatch, audio OOM, token bounds and CORS/no-auth.\n40\t  Map B07–B09/R01/C05; these are not MCP protocol flaws.\n41\t- **Artifact verification:** [Cosign GHSA-fx35-mq7g-6g98](https://github.com/sigstore/cosign/security/advisories/GHSA-fx35-mq7g-6g98),\n42\t  fixes 2.6.5/3.1.3, concerns legacy blob bundles; OCI image verification and modern\n43\t  protobuf bundles are explicitly unaffected. Map B10/C05 only where the path is used.\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"accd1a70-1d10-43fa-9d1f-e916b5212e32","uuid":"1671d5db-6523-43c1-8ec1-5c996795966f","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:18:38.729Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/skill/references/advisories.md","content":"# Dated advisory mapping\n\nChecked in BRACE's 2026-10-22 research. This is a starting index, not a live feed,\nexhaustive vulnerability database or minimum universally safe version list.\nRecheck the original record when reviewing a matching package. Read affected\nranges and prerequisites before claiming exposure. Record unresolved/opaque remote\nversions as unknown. Branches and prereleases require their own range handling.\n\n## MCP SDKs and server integration\n\n| Original advisory | Issue-specific fix/action to verify | BRACE mapping |\n|---|---|---|\n| [TS shared-server leak GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-jm63-cq47-j5jp), 2026-10-15 | Affected server 2.0.0–<2.3.0: upgrade to 2.3.0 and use fresh-server factories/proper per-request or per-session construction. The advisory excludes stdio and SDK 1.26+. | E06, R04 |\n| [TS validation exhaustion GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-46jr-4wx8-v453), 2026-10-15 | SDK 1.32.0/server 2.3.0 fix: explicitly configure `maxToolInputElements` (off by default) plus suitable body/parser limits. HTTP applicability matters. | B07, R01, E06 |\n| [TS body/batch exhaustion GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-f897-62hf-6gxq), 2026-10-15 | Fixes: SDK 1.30.1; server/node 2.1.0; hono 2.0.1. Patched body and batch defaults address different allocations; update each installed affected package. | B07, R01, E06 |\n| [TS issuer binding GHSA-6qxp-vccf-f47h](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6qxp-vccf-f47h), 2026-09-30 | SDK 1.31.0/client/core 2.2.0; configure `expectedIssuer`, bind/clear saved registrations and assess credential rotation/revocation. | B02–B04, E06 |\n| [TS audience GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rvq5-wwqv-78pq), 2026-10-05 | SDK 1.32.0/server 2.3.0/express 2.0.2/server-legacy 2.3.1. Configure `expectedResource` plus verified `AuthInfo.resource`, or enforce audience inside custom verification. Upgrade alone changes nothing. | B02, E06 |\n| [TS redirects GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw), 2026-10-02 | SDK 1.32.0/client 2.3.0. Review custom headers and 307/308 bodies; `redirectPolicy:'follow'` can restore exposure. Ordinary Authorization stripping is a distinct behavior. | B03, B09, E06, E12 |\n| [TS experimental tasks GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-22jm-h49p-29qw), 2026-10-02 | Affected SDK 1.24.0–<1.32.0: shared taskStore isolation fixed 1.32.0. 2.x excluded. Do not conflate legacy session-based tasks with redesigned Tasks handles. | E06, E09 |\n| [Python issuer binding GHSA-qx49-fqc8-xw99](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-qx49-fqc8-xw99), 2026-09-28 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0; unattended providers require `issuer=`, saved registrations need rebinding/clearing, deprecated provider needs migration. Assess exposure cleanup. | B02–B04, E06, E12 |\n| [Python audience GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-w4fh-qvv9-3v23), 2026-10-05 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0 plus `validate_token_resource=True`, correct `resource_server_url`, verified `AccessToken.resource`; custom audience checks may provide an alternative. | B02, E06 |\n| [Python schema refs GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-rwrf-2pqf-9j8j), 2026-09-28 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0 restricts output-schema resolution. Prior network/file resolution can stall or cause SSRF; advisory does not prove local file bytes reach the server. | R01, B09, E04, E06 |\n| [Python redirects GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-5h93-6whr-6q8j), 2026-10-02 | mcp 1.30.0/2.2.0; inspect cross-origin custom headers/bodies and any overridden redirect policy. Use final configured endpoints where appropriate. | B03, B09, E06 |\n| [Python body allocation GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-fmmv-w9g8-j3gc), 2026-09-30 | mcp 1.29.1/2.1.0; check body limits before allocation, including unauthenticated OAuth endpoints and proxy/application parsers. | B07, R01, E06 |\n| [Splunk SVD-2026-1004](https://advisory.splunk.com/advisories/SVD-2026-1004), 2026-10-07 | MCP Server 1.2.1; attacker tool-administrator changes destination, and another authorized executor runs it. Not an unauthenticated universal token leak. | B03, E03, E06, E12 |\n\n## Adjacent components: read only when present\n\n- **Hooks and local executable configuration:** [VS Code GHSA-w79w-rj9h-vg4f](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/security/advisories/GHSA-w79w-rj9h-vg4f),\n  fixed 1.132.1; map B05/B12/C02. A file-write tool may alter future execution.\n- **Filesystem tools and helper fetches:** [LangChain GHSA-gr75-jv2w-4656](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain/security/advisories/GHSA-gr75-jv2w-4656)\n  (glob/symlink/path boundaries) and [GHSA-r7w7-9xr2-qq2r](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain/security/advisories/GHSA-r7w7-9xr2-qq2r)\n  (image-counter DNS rebinding); map B09/B11/R01. Verify package-specific conditions.\n- **Persistent state:** [LangGraph namespace GHSA-6hmw-9432-67qq](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/security/advisories/GHSA-6hmw-9432-67qq),\n  Postgres 3.1.3/SQLite 3.1.2 with checkpoint 4.3.0; [auth-hook GHSA-4hm6-w6qq-w73v](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/security/advisories/GHSA-4hm6-w6qq-w73v),\n  API 0.15.0; [checkpoint revival GHSA-fjqc-hq36-qh5p](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/security/advisories/GHSA-fjqc-hq36-qh5p),\n  checkpoint 4.1.1. Map R04–R06/R14/A04; do not infer safe decoding from a signature.\n- **Model/preprocessing services:** inspect applicable [vLLM advisories](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/security/advisories),\n  including October 20 image dispatch, audio OOM, token bounds and CORS/no-auth.\n  Map B07–B09/R01/C05; these are not MCP protocol flaws.\n- **Artifact verification:** [Cosign GHSA-fx35-mq7g-6g98](https://github.com/sigstore/cosign/security/advisories/GHSA-fx35-mq7g-6g98),\n  fixes 2.6.5/3.1.3, concerns legacy blob bundles; OCI image verification and modern\n  protobuf bundles are explicitly unaffected. Map B10/C05 only where the path is used.\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014JGfKD4RqGGDrPTxnWDo9i","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgQgHDLKmUCmBwJczCxap","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DBuqZkqrXAYCfW7cMCrEqZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/repo/src/filesystem/server.ts"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5267,"cache_read_input_tokens":16762,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5267},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"accd1a70-1d10-43fa-9d1f-e916b5212e32","uuid":"f32e306d-b77f-4916-a3cd-c82c34c10a93","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:18:41.154Z","request_id":"req_011CgQgHD2yVrdG8mFcA1tHV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DBuqZkqrXAYCfW7cMCrEqZ":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/repo/src/filesystem/server.ts"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DBuqZkqrXAYCfW7cMCrEqZ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// The filesystem server, built per call by createServer(). index.ts is the\n2\t// stdio entry point that resolves the allowed directories from argv and hands\n3\t// them here. Building the server in a factory, rather than at module load,\n4\t// lets the tests drive a real Client against it in-process over an in-memory\n5\t// transport (#4854), and holding the allow-list in this closure rather than in\n6\t// a module global lets two instances coexist in one process.\n7\timport { McpServer } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/server\";\n8\timport type { Root } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/server\";\n9\timport fs from \"fs/promises\";\n10\timport { createReadStream } from \"fs\";\n11\timport path from \"path\";\n12\timport { pathToFileURL } from \"url\";\n13\timport { z } from \"zod\";\n14\timport { minimatch } from \"minimatch\";\n15\timport { getValidRootDirectories } from \"./roots-utils.js\";\n16\timport {\n17\t  formatSize,\n18\t  validatePath,\n19\t  getFileStats,\n20\t  readFileContent,\n21\t  writeFileContent,\n22\t  createDirectory,\n23\t  moveFile,\n24\t  searchFilesWithValidation,\n25\t  applyFileEdits,\n26\t  tailFile,\n27\t  headFile,\n28\t} from \"./lib.js\";\n29\timport { SERVER_VERSION } from \"./version.js\";\n30\t\n31\t// The JSON Schema dialect every advertised tool schema declares (#4841). The\n32\t// SDK 1.x tools/list handler rendered zod schemas with zod's draft-07 target and\n33\t// stamped \"$schema\": draft-07 on each one, which validators that accept only\n34\t// 2020-12 (the dialect MCP assumes for tool schemas) reject outright. Setting\n35\t// $schema in the root object's zod metadata overrode that stamp. SDK v2 stamps\n36\t// 2020-12 itself, so the label is now the SDK's default as well. The schemas\n37\t// below use nothing whose draft-07 and 2020-12 renderings differ (no tuples),\n38\t// so the label is the only change; server-tools.test.ts fails if a draft-07-only\n39\t// keyword ever appears under it.\n40\tconst JSON_SCHEMA_DIALECT = \"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\";\n41\t\n42\t/** A tool's input or output shape as an object schema declaring 2020-12. */\n43\tfunction jsonSchema2020<Shape extends z.ZodRawShape>(shape: Shape) {\n44\t  return z.object(shape).meta({ $schema: JSON_SCHEMA_DIALECT });\n45\t}\n46\t\n47\t// Schema definitions\n48\tconst ReadTextFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n49\t  path: z.string(),\n50\t  tail: z\n51\t    .number()\n52\t    .optional()\n53\t    .describe(\"If provided, returns only the last N lines of the file\"),\n54\t  head: z\n55\t    .number()\n56\t    .optional()\n57\t    .describe(\"If provided, returns only the first N lines of the file\"),\n58\t});\n59\t\n60\tconst ReadMediaFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n61\t  path: z.string(),\n62\t});\n63\t\n64\tconst ReadMultipleFilesArgsSchema = z.object({\n65\t  paths: z\n66\t    .array(z.string())\n67\t    .min(1)\n68\t    .describe(\n69\t      \"Array of file paths to read. Each path must be a string pointing to a valid file within allowed directories.\",\n70\t    ),\n71\t});\n72\t\n73\tconst WriteFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n74\t  path: z.string(),\n75\t  content: z.string(),\n76\t});\n77\t\n78\tconst EditOperation = z.object({\n79\t  oldText: z.string().describe(\"Text to search for - must match exactly\"),\n80\t  newText: z.string().describe(\"Text to replace with\"),\n81\t});\n82\t\n83\tconst EditFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n84\t  path: z.string(),\n85\t  edits: z.array(EditOperation),\n86\t  dryRun: z\n87\t    .boolean()\n88\t    .default(false)\n89\t    .describe(\"Preview changes using git-style diff format\"),\n90\t});\n91\t\n92\tconst CreateDirectoryArgsSchema = z.object({\n93\t  path: z.string(),\n94\t});\n95\t\n96\tconst ListDirectoryArgsSchema = z.object({\n97\t  path: z.string(),\n98\t});\n99\t\n100\tconst ListDirectoryWithSizesArgsSchema = z.object({\n101\t  path: z.string(),\n102\t  sortBy: z\n103\t    .enum([\"name\", \"size\"])\n104\t    .optional()\n105\t    .default(\"name\")\n106\t    .describe(\"Sort entries by name or size\"),\n107\t});\n108\t\n109\tconst DirectoryTreeArgsSchema = z.object({\n110\t  path: z.string(),\n111\t  excludePatterns: z.array(z.string()).optional().default([]),\n112\t});\n113\t\n114\tconst MoveFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n115\t  source: z.string(),\n116\t  destination: z.string(),\n117\t});\n118\t\n119\tconst SearchFilesArgsSchema = z.object({\n120\t  path: z.string(),\n121\t  pattern: z.string(),\n122\t  excludePatterns: z.array(z.string()).optional().default([]),\n123\t});\n124\t\n125\tconst GetFileInfoArgsSchema = z.object({\n126\t  path: z.string(),\n127\t});\n128\t\n129\t// Reads a file as a stream of buffers, concatenates them, and then encodes\n130\t// the result to a Base64 string. This is a memory-efficient way to handle\n131\t// binary data from a stream before the final encoding.\n132\tasync function readFileAsBase64Stream(filePath: string): Promise<string> {\n133\t  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n134\t    const stream = createReadStream(filePath);\n135\t    const chunks: Buffer[] = [];\n136\t    stream.on(\"data\", (chunk) => {\n137\t      chunks.push(chunk as Buffer);\n138\t    });\n139\t    stream.on(\"end\", () => {\n140\t      const finalBuffer = Buffer.concat(chunks);\n141\t      resolve(finalBuffer.toString(\"base64\"));\n142\t    });\n143\t    stream.on(\"error\", (err) => reject(err));\n144\t  });\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/**\n148\t * Why the server cannot operate when it has no allowed directories from either\n149\t * the command line or the client's Roots (#4992).\n150\t */\n151\texport const NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_ERROR =\n152\t  \"Server cannot operate: No allowed directories available. Server was started without command-line directories and client does not support MCP roots protocol. Please either: 1) Start server with directory arguments, or 2) Use a client that supports MCP roots protocol and provides valid root directories.\";\n153\t\n154\t/**\n155\t * Why the server cannot operate on a 2026-07-28 connection when it was started\n156\t * with no allowed directories. That revision has no initialize handshake and\n157\t * no server-to-client roots/list, so the client's Roots cannot supply them\n158\t * (Roots is deprecated as of 2026-07-28); only the command line can (#4852).\n159\t */\n160\texport const NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_MODERN_ERROR =\n161\t  \"Server cannot operate: No allowed directories available. Server was started without command-line directories, and on protocol version 2026-07-28 the client cannot provide them through MCP Roots. Restart the server with the directories it may access as command-line arguments.\";\n162\t\n163\texport interface ServerOptions {\n164\t  /**\n165\t   * The protocol era the server will serve (default `legacy`). On a\n166\t   * 2025-era connection the client's Roots, when it supports them, replace\n167\t   * the command-line directories after initialize. A 2026-07-28 connection\n168\t   * has no initialize and no roots/list, so it is limited to the\n169\t   * command-line directories.\n170\t   */\n171\t  era?: \"legacy\" | \"modern\";\n172\t  /**\n173\t   * Called after the server has closed the connection because it cannot\n174\t   * operate: no directories were given and the client does not support\n175\t   * Roots (#4992). The stdio entry point exits the process from here.\n176\t   */\n177\t  onCannotOperate?: (error: Error) => void;\n178\t}\n179\t\n180\t/**\n181\t * Build a filesystem server that may touch only `initialAllowedDirectories`\n182\t * (already resolved and normalized by the caller), until the client's Roots\n183\t * replace them on a 2025-era connection.\n184\t */\n185\texport function createServer(\n186\t  initialAllowedDirectories: string[],\n187\t  options: ServerOptions = {},\n188\t): McpServer {\n189\t  let allowedDirectories = [...initialAllowedDirectories];\n190\t  // True once the client's roots have replaced the command-line directories.\n191\t  // From then on a roots update replaces them even with none, so a client that\n192\t  // withdraws its roots withdraws the server's access too (#5094). Until then an\n193\t  // update with no valid roots keeps the command-line directories.\n194\t  let rootsInForce = false;\n195\t  // Each roots refresh (the initial load and every roots/list_changed) takes\n196\t  // the next generation before it asks for roots. The SDK runs notification\n197\t  // handlers without waiting for earlier ones, so answers can come back out of\n198\t  // order; one is applied only if no newer refresh has started since, so a\n199\t  // stale answer cannot undo a newer update, such as a revocation (#5097).\n200\t  let rootsGeneration = 0;\n201\t\n202\t  const server = new McpServer({\n203\t    name: \"secure-filesystem-server\",\n204\t    version: SERVER_VERSION,\n205\t  });\n206\t\n207\t  // The barrier every tool call waits on, so a call that arrives while roots are\n208\t  // being fetched is checked against the roots that come back rather than the\n209\t  // ones being replaced: the command-line directories while the initial\n210\t  // roots/list is outstanding (#3204), or a root the client has just withdrawn\n211\t  // while a roots/list_changed refresh is pending (#5101). Each refresh (the\n212\t  // initial load included) installs a new barrier before it sends roots/list,\n213\t  // and settles it when it finishes, successfully or not. Installing one also\n214\t  // settles the barrier it replaces, so calls waiting on an older refresh wake\n215\t  // at once and move on to the newer one instead of waiting for the older\n216\t  // one's answer (#5097, #5101).\n217\t  let barrier = { settled: Promise.resolve(), settle: () => {} };\n218\t  function installBarrier(): () => void {\n219\t    let settle!: () => void;\n220\t    const settled = new Promise<void>((resolve) => (settle = resolve));\n221\t    const replaced = barrier;\n222\t    barrier = { settled, settle };\n223\t    replaced.settle();\n224\t    return settle;\n225\t  }\n226\t  const registerTool = server.registerTool.bind(server);\n227\t  server.registerTool = ((\n228\t    name: string,\n229\t    config: Parameters<typeof registerTool>[1],\n230\t    handler: (...args: unknown[]) => unknown,\n231\t  ) =>\n232\t    registerTool(name, config, (async (...args: unknown[]) => {\n233\t      let awaited: typeof barrier;\n234\t      do {\n235\t        awaited = barrier;\n236\t        await awaited.settled;\n237\t      } while (awaited !== barrier);\n238\t      // A 2025-era session with no directories closes at initialize (#4992).\n239\t      // A 2026-07-28 one has no initialize to close at, and its client cannot\n240\t      // add directories, so every call says why it cannot run.\n241\t      if (options.era === \"modern\" && allowedDirectories.length === 0) {\n242\t        return {\n243\t          content: [\n244\t            { type: \"text\", text: NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_MODERN_ERROR },\n245\t          ],\n246\t          isError: true,\n247\t        };\n248\t      }\n249\t      return handler(...args);\n250\t    }) as never)) as typeof server.registerTool;\n251\t\n252\t  // Tool registrations\n253\t\n254\t  // read_file (deprecated) and read_text_file\n255\t  const readTextFileHandler = async (\n256\t    args: z.infer<typeof ReadTextFileArgsSchema>,\n257\t  ) => {\n258\t    const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n259\t\n260\t    if (args.head && args.tail) {\n261\t      throw new Error(\n262\t        \"Cannot specify both head and tail parameters simultaneously\",\n263\t      );\n264\t    }\n265\t\n266\t    let content: string;\n267\t    if (args.tail) {\n268\t      content = await tailFile(validPath, args.tail);\n269\t    } else if (args.head) {\n270\t      content = await headFile(validPath, args.head);\n271\t    } else {\n272\t      content = await readFileContent(validPath);\n273\t    }\n274\t\n275\t    return {\n276\t      content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: content }],\n277\t      structuredContent: { content },\n278\t    };\n279\t  };\n280\t\n281\t  server.registerTool(\n282\t    \"read_file\",\n283\t    {\n284\t      title: \"Read File (Deprecated)\",\n285\t      description:\n286\t        \"Read the complete contents of a file as text. DEPRECATED: Use read_text_file instead.\",\n287\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ReadTextFileArgsSchema.shape),\n288\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n289\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n290\t    },\n291\t    readTextFileHandler,\n292\t  );\n293\t\n294\t  server.registerTool(\n295\t    \"read_text_file\",\n296\t    {\n297\t      title: \"Read Text File\",\n298\t      description:\n299\t        \"Read the complete contents of a file from the file system as text. \" +\n300\t        \"Handles various text encodings and provides detailed error messages \" +\n301\t        \"if the file cannot be read. Use this tool when you need to examine \" +\n302\t        \"the contents of a single file. Use the 'head' parameter to read only \" +\n303\t        \"the first N lines of a file, or the 'tail' parameter to read only \" +\n304\t        \"the last N lines of a file. Operates on the file as text regardless of extension. \" +\n305\t        \"Only works within allowed directories.\",\n306\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020({\n307\t        path: z.string(),\n308\t        tail: z\n309\t          .number()\n310\t          .optional()\n311\t          .describe(\"If provided, returns only the last N lines of the file\"),\n312\t        head: z\n313\t          .number()\n314\t          .optional()\n315\t          .describe(\"If provided, returns only the first N lines of the file\"),\n316\t      }),\n317\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n318\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n319\t    },\n320\t    readTextFileHandler,\n321\t  );\n322\t\n323\t  server.registerTool(\n324\t    \"read_media_file\",\n325\t    {\n326\t      title: \"Read Media File\",\n327\t      description:\n328\t        \"Read a file and return it as a base64-encoded content block with its MIME type. \" +\n329\t        \"Image and audio files are returned as image/audio content; any other file type is \" +\n330\t        \"returned as an embedded resource. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n331\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ReadMediaFileArgsSchema.shape),\n332\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({\n333\t        content: z.array(\n334\t          z.union([\n335\t            z.object({\n336\t              type: z.enum([\"image\", \"audio\"]),\n337\t              data: z.string(),\n338\t              mimeType: z.string(),\n339\t            }),\n340\t            z.object({\n341\t              type: z.literal(\"resource\"),\n342\t              resource: z.object({\n343\t                uri: z.string(),\n344\t                // Optional, matching the SDK's BlobResourceContents shape (the handler always sets it).\n345\t                mimeType: z.string().optional(),\n346\t                blob: z.string(),\n347\t              }),\n348\t            }),\n349\t          ]),\n350\t        ),\n351\t      }),\n352\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n353\t    },\n354\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof ReadMediaFileArgsSchema>) => {\n355\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n356\t      const extension = path.extname(validPath).toLowerCase();\n357\t      const mimeTypes: Record<string, string> = {\n358\t        \".png\": \"image/png\",\n359\t        \".jpg\": \"image/jpeg\",\n360\t        \".jpeg\": \"image/jpeg\",\n361\t        \".gif\": \"image/gif\",\n362\t        \".webp\": \"image/webp\",\n363\t        \".bmp\": \"image/bmp\",\n364\t        \".svg\": \"image/svg+xml\",\n365\t        \".mp3\": \"audio/mpeg\",\n366\t        \".wav\": \"audio/wav\",\n367\t        \".ogg\": \"audio/ogg\",\n368\t        \".flac\": \"audio/flac\",\n369\t      };\n370\t      const mimeType = mimeTypes[extension] || \"application/octet-stream\";\n371\t      const data = await readFileAsBase64Stream(validPath);\n372\t\n373\t      // Map the MIME type to a valid MCP content block. The spec only allows\n374\t      // text, image, audio, resource_link, and resource — so non-image/audio\n375\t      // binaries are returned as an embedded resource (NOT type:\"blob\", which the\n376\t      // SDK content-block union rejects on schema validation).\n377\t      const contentItem = mimeType.startsWith(\"image/\")\n378\t        ? { type: \"image\" as const, data, mimeType }\n379\t        : mimeType.startsWith(\"audio/\")\n380\t          ? { type: \"audio\" as const, data, mimeType }\n381\t          : {\n382\t              type: \"resource\" as const,\n383\t              resource: {\n384\t                uri: pathToFileURL(validPath).href,\n385\t                mimeType,\n386\t                blob: data,\n387\t              },\n388\t            };\n389\t      return {\n390\t        content: [contentItem],\n391\t        structuredContent: { content: [contentItem] },\n392\t      };\n393\t    },\n394\t  );\n395\t\n396\t  server.registerTool(\n397\t    \"read_multiple_files\",\n398\t    {\n399\t      title: \"Read Multiple Files\",\n400\t      description:\n401\t        \"Read the contents of multiple files simultaneously. This is more \" +\n402\t        \"efficient than reading files one by one when you need to analyze \" +\n403\t        \"or compare multiple files. Each file's content is returned with its \" +\n404\t        \"path as a reference. Failed reads for individual files won't stop \" +\n405\t        \"the entire operation. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n406\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ReadMultipleFilesArgsSchema.shape),\n407\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n408\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n409\t    },\n410\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof ReadMultipleFilesArgsSchema>) => {\n411\t      const results = await Promise.all(\n412\t        args.paths.map(async (filePath: string) => {\n413\t          try {\n414\t            const validPath = await validatePath(filePath, allowedDirectories);\n415\t            const content = await readFileContent(validPath);\n416\t            return `${filePath}:\\n${content}\\n`;\n417\t          } catch (error) {\n418\t            const errorMessage =\n419\t              error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);\n420\t            return `${filePath}: Error - ${errorMessage}`;\n421\t          }\n422\t        }),\n423\t      );\n424\t      const text = results.join(\"\\n---\\n\");\n425\t      return {\n426\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n427\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n428\t      };\n429\t    },\n430\t  );\n431\t\n432\t  server.registerTool(\n433\t    \"write_file\",\n434\t    {\n435\t      title: \"Write File\",\n436\t      description:\n437\t        \"Create a new file or completely overwrite an existing file with new content. \" +\n438\t        \"Use with caution as it will overwrite existing files without warning. \" +\n439\t        \"Handles text content with proper encoding. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n440\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(WriteFileArgsSchema.shape),\n441\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n442\t      annotations: {\n443\t        readOnlyHint: false,\n444\t        idempotentHint: true,\n445\t        destructiveHint: true,\n446\t        openWorldHint: false,\n447\t      },\n448\t    },\n449\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof WriteFileArgsSchema>) => {\n450\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n451\t      await writeFileContent(validPath, args.content);\n452\t      const text = `Successfully wrote to ${args.path}`;\n453\t      return {\n454\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n455\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n456\t      };\n457\t    },\n458\t  );\n459\t\n460\t  server.registerTool(\n461\t    \"edit_file\",\n462\t    {\n463\t      title: \"Edit File\",\n464\t      description:\n465\t        \"Make line-based edits to a text file. Each edit replaces exact line sequences \" +\n466\t        \"with new content. Returns a git-style diff showing the changes made. \" +\n467\t        \"Only works within allowed directories.\",\n468\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(EditFileArgsSchema.shape),\n469\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n470\t      annotations: {\n471\t        readOnlyHint: false,\n472\t        idempotentHint: false,\n473\t        destructiveHint: true,\n474\t        openWorldHint: false,\n475\t      },\n476\t    },\n477\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof EditFileArgsSchema>) => {\n478\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n479\t      const result = await applyFileEdits(validPath, args.edits, args.dryRun);\n480\t      return {\n481\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: result }],\n482\t        structuredContent: { content: result },\n483\t      };\n484\t    },\n485\t  );\n486\t\n487\t  server.registerTool(\n488\t    \"create_directory\",\n489\t    {\n490\t      title: \"Create Directory\",\n491\t      description:\n492\t        \"Create a new directory or ensure a directory exists. Can create multiple \" +\n493\t        \"nested directories in one operation. If the directory already exists, \" +\n494\t        \"this operation will succeed silently. Perfect for setting up directory \" +\n495\t        \"structures for projects or ensuring required paths exist. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n496\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(CreateDirectoryArgsSchema.shape),\n497\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n498\t      annotations: {\n499\t        readOnlyHint: false,\n500\t        idempotentHint: true,\n501\t        destructiveHint: false,\n502\t        openWorldHint: false,\n503\t      },\n504\t    },\n505\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof CreateDirectoryArgsSchema>) => {\n506\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n507\t      await createDirectory(validPath);\n508\t      const text = `Successfully created directory ${args.path}`;\n509\t      return {\n510\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n511\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n512\t      };\n513\t    },\n514\t  );\n515\t\n516\t  server.registerTool(\n517\t    \"list_directory\",\n518\t    {\n519\t      title: \"List Directory\",\n520\t      description:\n521\t        \"Get a detailed listing of all files and directories in a specified path. \" +\n522\t        \"Results clearly distinguish between files and directories with [FILE] and [DIR] \" +\n523\t        \"prefixes. This tool is essential for understanding directory structure and \" +\n524\t        \"finding specific files within a directory. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n525\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ListDirectoryArgsSchema.shape),\n526\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n527\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n528\t    },\n529\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof ListDirectoryArgsSchema>) => {\n530\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n531\t      const entries = await fs.readdir(validPath, { withFileTypes: true });\n532\t      const formatted = entries\n533\t        .map(\n534\t          (entry) =>\n535\t            `${entry.isDirectory() ? \"[DIR]\" : \"[FILE]\"} ${entry.name}`,\n536\t        )\n537\t        .join(\"\\n\");\n538\t      return {\n539\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: formatted }],\n540\t        structuredContent: { content: formatted },\n541\t      };\n542\t    },\n543\t  );\n544\t\n545\t  server.registerTool(\n546\t    \"list_directory_with_sizes\",\n547\t    {\n548\t      title: \"List Directory with Sizes\",\n549\t      description:\n550\t        \"Get a detailed listing of all files and directories in a specified path, including sizes. \" +\n551\t        \"Results clearly distinguish between files and directories with [FILE] and [DIR] \" +\n552\t        \"prefixes. This tool is useful for understanding directory structure and \" +\n553\t        \"finding specific files within a directory. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n554\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ListDirectoryWithSizesArgsSchema.shape),\n555\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n556\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n557\t    },\n558\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof ListDirectoryWithSizesArgsSchema>) => {\n559\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n560\t      const entries = await fs.readdir(validPath, { withFileTypes: true });\n561\t\n562\t      // Get detailed information for each entry\n563\t      const detailedEntries = await Promise.all(\n564\t        entries.map(async (entry) => {\n565\t          const entryPath = path.join(validPath, entry.name);\n566\t          try {\n567\t            // fs.stat follows symlinks, so validate each entry first: a\n568\t            // symlink whose target is outside the allowed directories is\n569\t            // listed with no size rather than the target's.\n570\t            const validEntryPath = await validatePath(\n571\t              entryPath,\n572\t              allowedDirectories,\n573\t            );\n574\t            const stats = await fs.stat(validEntryPath);\n575\t            return {\n576\t              name: entry.name,\n577\t              isDirectory: entry.isDirectory(),\n578\t              size: stats.size,\n579\t              mtime: stats.mtime,\n580\t            };\n581\t          } catch {\n582\t            return {\n583\t              name: entry.name,\n584\t              isDirectory: entry.isDirectory(),\n585\t              size: 0,\n586\t              mtime: new Date(0),\n587\t            };\n588\t          }\n589\t        }),\n590\t      );\n591\t\n592\t      // Sort entries based on sortBy parameter\n593\t      const sortedEntries = [...detailedEntries].sort((a, b) => {\n594\t        if (args.sortBy === \"size\") {\n595\t          return b.size - a.size; // Descending by size\n596\t        }\n597\t        // Default sort by name\n598\t        return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);\n599\t      });\n600\t\n601\t      // Format the output\n602\t      const formattedEntries = sortedEntries.map(\n603\t        (entry) =>\n604\t          `${entry.isDirectory ? \"[DIR]\" : \"[FILE]\"} ${entry.name.padEnd(30)} ${\n605\t            entry.isDirectory ? \"\" : formatSize(entry.size).padStart(10)\n606\t          }`,\n607\t      );\n608\t\n609\t      // Add summary\n610\t      const totalFiles = detailedEntries.filter((e) => !e.isDirectory).length;\n611\t      const totalDirs = detailedEntries.filter((e) => e.isDirectory).length;\n612\t      const totalSize = detailedEntries.reduce(\n613\t        (sum, entry) => sum + (entry.isDirectory ? 0 : entry.size),\n614\t        0,\n615\t      );\n616\t\n617\t      const summary = [\n618\t        \"\",\n619\t        `Total: ${totalFiles} files, ${totalDirs} directories`,\n620\t        `Combined size: ${formatSize(totalSize)}`,\n621\t      ];\n622\t\n623\t      const text = [...formattedEntries, ...summary].join(\"\\n\");\n624\t      const contentBlock = { type: \"text\" as const, text };\n625\t      return {\n626\t        content: [contentBlock],\n627\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n628\t      };\n629\t    },\n630\t  );\n631\t\n632\t  server.registerTool(\n633\t    \"directory_tree\",\n634\t    {\n635\t      title: \"Directory Tree\",\n636\t      description:\n637\t        \"Get a recursive tree view of files and directories as a JSON structure. \" +\n638\t        \"Each entry includes 'name', 'type' (file/directory), and 'children' for directories. \" +\n639\t        \"Files have no children array, while directories always have a children array (which may be empty). \" +\n640\t        \"The output is formatted with 2-space indentation for readability. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n641\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(DirectoryTreeArgsSchema.shape),\n642\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n643\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n644\t    },\n645\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof DirectoryTreeArgsSchema>) => {\n646\t      interface TreeEntry {\n647\t        name: string;\n648\t        type: \"file\" | \"directory\";\n649\t        children?: TreeEntry[];\n650\t      }\n651\t      const rootPath = args.path;\n652\t\n653\t      async function buildTree(\n654\t        currentPath: string,\n655\t        excludePatterns: string[] = [],\n656\t      ): Promise<TreeEntry[]> {\n657\t        const validPath = await validatePath(currentPath, allowedDirectories);\n658\t        const entries = await fs.readdir(validPath, { withFileTypes: true });\n659\t        const result: TreeEntry[] = [];\n660\t\n661\t        for (const entry of entries) {\n662\t          const relativePath = path.relative(\n663\t            rootPath,\n664\t            path.join(currentPath, entry.name),\n665\t          );\n666\t          const shouldExclude = excludePatterns.some((pattern) => {\n667\t            if (pattern.includes(\"*\")) {\n668\t              return minimatch(relativePath, pattern, { dot: true });\n669\t            }\n670\t            // For files: match exact name or as part of path\n671\t            // For directories: match as directory path\n672\t            return (\n673\t              minimatch(relativePath, pattern, { dot: true }) ||\n674\t              minimatch(relativePath, `**/${pattern}`, { dot: true }) ||\n675\t              minimatch(relativePath, `**/${pattern}/**`, { dot: true })\n676\t            );\n677\t          });\n678\t          if (shouldExclude) continue;\n679\t\n680\t          const entryData: TreeEntry = {\n681\t            name: entry.name,\n682\t            type: entry.isDirectory() ? \"directory\" : \"file\",\n683\t          };\n684\t\n685\t          if (entry.isDirectory()) {\n686\t            const subPath = path.join(currentPath, entry.name);\n687\t            entryData.children = await buildTree(subPath, excludePatterns);\n688\t          }\n689\t\n690\t          result.push(entryData);\n691\t        }\n692\t\n693\t        return result;\n694\t      }\n695\t\n696\t      const treeData = await buildTree(rootPath, args.excludePatterns);\n697\t      const text = JSON.stringify(treeData, null, 2);\n698\t      const contentBlock = { type: \"text\" as const, text };\n699\t      return {\n700\t        content: [contentBlock],\n701\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n702\t      };\n703\t    },\n704\t  );\n705\t\n706\t  server.registerTool(\n707\t    \"move_file\",\n708\t    {\n709\t      title: \"Move File\",\n710\t      description:\n711\t        \"Move or rename files and directories. Can move files between directories \" +\n712\t        \"and rename them in a single operation. If the destination exists, the \" +\n713\t        \"operation will fail. Works across different directories and can be used \" +\n714\t        \"for simple renaming within the same directory. Both source and destination must be within allowed directories.\",\n715\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(MoveFileArgsSchema.shape),\n716\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n717\t      annotations: {\n718\t        readOnlyHint: false,\n719\t        idempotentHint: false,\n720\t        destructiveHint: true,\n721\t        openWorldHint: false,\n722\t      },\n723\t    },\n724\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof MoveFileArgsSchema>) => {\n725\t      const validSourcePath = await validatePath(\n726\t        args.source,\n727\t        allowedDirectories,\n728\t      );\n729\t      const validDestPath = await validatePath(\n730\t        args.destination,\n731\t        allowedDirectories,\n732\t      );\n733\t      await moveFile(validSourcePath, validDestPath);\n734\t      const text = `Successfully moved ${args.source} to ${args.destination}`;\n735\t      const contentBlock = { type: \"text\" as const, text };\n736\t      return {\n737\t        content: [contentBlock],\n738\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n739\t      };\n740\t    },\n741\t  );\n742\t\n743\t  server.registerTool(\n744\t    \"search_files\",\n745\t    {\n746\t      title: \"Search Files\",\n747\t      description:\n748\t        \"Recursively search for files and directories matching a pattern. \" +\n749\t        \"The patterns should be glob-style patterns that match paths relative to the working directory. \" +\n750\t        \"Use pattern like '*.ext' to match files in current directory, and '**/*.ext' to match files in all subdirectories. \" +\n751\t        \"Returns full paths to all matching items. Great for finding files when you don't know their exact location. \" +\n752\t        \"Only searches within allowed directories.\",\n753\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(SearchFilesArgsSchema.shape),\n754\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n755\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n756\t    },\n757\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof SearchFilesArgsSchema>) => {\n758\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n759\t      const results = await searchFilesWithValidation(\n760\t        validPath,\n761\t        args.pattern,\n762\t        allowedDirectories,\n763\t        { excludePatterns: args.excludePatterns },\n764\t      );\n765\t      const text = results.length > 0 ? results.join(\"\\n\") : \"No matches found\";\n766\t      return {\n767\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n768\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n769\t      };\n770\t    },\n771\t  );\n772\t\n773\t  server.registerTool(\n774\t    \"get_file_info\",\n775\t    {\n776\t      title: \"Get File Info\",\n777\t      description:\n778\t        \"Retrieve detailed metadata about a file or directory. Returns comprehensive \" +\n779\t        \"information including size, creation time, last modified time, permissions, \" +\n780\t        \"and type. This tool is perfect for understanding file characteristics \" +\n781\t        \"without reading the actual content. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n782\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(GetFileInfoArgsSchema.shape),\n783\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n784\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n785\t    },\n786\t    async (args: z.infer<typeof GetFileInfoArgsSchema>) => {\n787\t      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n788\t      const info = await getFileStats(validPath);\n789\t      const text = Object.entries(info)\n790\t        .map(([key, value]) => `${key}: ${value}`)\n791\t        .join(\"\\n\");\n792\t      return {\n793\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n794\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n795\t      };\n796\t    },\n797\t  );\n798\t\n799\t  server.registerTool(\n800\t    \"list_allowed_directories\",\n801\t    {\n802\t      title: \"List Allowed Directories\",\n803\t      description:\n804\t        \"Returns the list of directories that this server is allowed to access. \" +\n805\t        \"Subdirectories within these allowed directories are also accessible. \" +\n806\t        \"Use this to understand which directories and their nested paths are available \" +\n807\t        \"before trying to access files.\",\n808\t      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020({}),\n809\t      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n810\t      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n811\t    },\n812\t    async () => {\n813\t      const text = `Allowed directories:\\n${allowedDirectories.join(\"\\n\")}`;\n814\t      return {\n815\t        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n816\t        structuredContent: { content: text },\n817\t      };\n818\t    },\n819\t  );\n820\t\n821\t  // Updates allowed directories based on MCP client roots, unless a newer\n822\t  // refresh started after this one's `generation` was taken (#5097).\n823\t  async function updateAllowedDirectoriesFromRoots(\n824\t    requestedRoots: Root[],\n825\t    generation: number,\n826\t  ) {\n827\t    const validatedRootDirs = await getValidRootDirectories(requestedRoots);\n828\t    if (generation !== rootsGeneration) {\n829\t      console.error(\n830\t        \"Discarded a stale roots/list answer: a newer roots update has started\",\n831\t      );\n832\t      return;\n833\t    }\n834\t    if (validatedRootDirs.length > 0) {\n835\t      allowedDirectories = [...validatedRootDirs];\n836\t      rootsInForce = true;\n837\t      console.error(\n838\t        `Updated allowed directories from MCP roots: ${validatedRootDirs.length} valid directories`,\n839\t      );\n840\t    } else if (rootsInForce) {\n841\t      allowedDirectories = [];\n842\t      console.error(\n843\t        \"No valid root directories provided by client; access revoked until it exposes a root again\",\n844\t      );\n845\t    } else {\n846\t      console.error(\"No valid root directories provided by client\");\n847\t    }\n848\t  }\n849\t\n850\t  // Handles dynamic roots updates during runtime, when client sends \"roots/list_changed\" notification, server fetches the updated roots and replaces all allowed directories with the new roots.\n851\t  server.server.setNotificationHandler(\n852\t    \"notifications/roots/list_changed\",\n853\t    async () => {\n854\t      const generation = ++rootsGeneration;\n855\t      const settle = installBarrier();\n856\t      try {\n857\t        // Request the updated roots list from the client\n858\t        const response = await server.server.listRoots();\n859\t        /* v8 ignore else -- the SDK validates the roots/list result against ListRootsResultSchema, which requires roots, so the implicit else cannot run */\n860\t        if (response && \"roots\" in response) {\n861\t          await updateAllowedDirectoriesFromRoots(response.roots, generation);\n862\t        }\n863\t      } catch (error) {\n864\t        console.error(\n865\t          \"Failed to request roots from client:\",\n866\t          error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n867\t        );\n868\t      } finally {\n869\t        settle();\n870\t      }\n871\t    },\n872\t  );\n873\t\n874\t  // Handles post-initialization setup, specifically checking for and fetching\n875\t  // MCP roots. Only a 2025-era connection initializes, so this never runs on\n876\t  // 2026-07-28, where Roots is unavailable (and deprecated). The initial load claims its generation and installs its barrier\n877\t  // here, synchronously, before any roots/list_changed handled afterwards can\n878\t  // claim a newer one.\n879\t  server.server.oninitialized = () => {\n880\t    const generation = ++rootsGeneration;\n881\t    const settle = installBarrier();\n882\t    // Not awaited: oninitialized is synchronous. Tool calls wait on the\n883\t    // barrier, which settles when initialize() finishes; it never rejects.\n884\t    void initialize(generation).finally(settle);\n885\t  };\n886\t\n887\t  async function initialize(generation: number): Promise<void> {\n888\t    const clientCapabilities = server.server.getClientCapabilities();\n889\t\n890\t    if (clientCapabilities?.roots) {\n891\t      try {\n892\t        const response = await server.server.listRoots();\n893\t        /* v8 ignore else -- the SDK validates the roots/list result against ListRootsResultSchema, which requires roots, so the else cannot run */\n894\t        if (response && \"roots\" in response) {\n895\t          await updateAllowedDirectoriesFromRoots(response.roots, generation);\n896\t        } else {\n897\t          console.error(\n898\t            \"Client returned no roots set, keeping current settings\",\n899\t          );\n900\t        }\n901\t      } catch (error) {\n902\t        console.error(\n903\t          \"Failed to request initial roots from client:\",\n904\t          error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n905\t        );\n906\t      }\n907\t    } else {\n908\t      if (allowedDirectories.length > 0) {\n909\t        console.error(\n910\t          \"Client does not support MCP Roots, using allowed directories set from server args:\",\n911\t          allowedDirectories,\n912\t        );\n913\t      } else {\n914\t        // Nothing could ever be allowed in this session, so fail visibly\n915\t        // rather than serve every call with \"Access denied\" (#4992): log the\n916\t        // reason and close the connection. Throwing here would only reach the\n917\t        // SDK's onerror, which the client never sees.\n918\t        const error = new Error(NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_ERROR);\n919\t        console.error(`Error: ${error.message}`);\n920\t        await server.close();\n921\t        options.onCannotOperate?.(error);\n922\t      }\n923\t    }\n924\t  }\n925\t\n926\t  return server;\n927\t}\n928\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"accd1a70-1d10-43fa-9d1f-e916b5212e32","uuid":"8c1ef42b-95bb-42b1-9129-b8014877f4f2","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:18:41.449Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-real-mcp-review-josy6fqr/repo/src/filesystem/server.ts","content":"// The filesystem server, built per call by createServer(). index.ts is the\n// stdio entry point that resolves the allowed directories from argv and hands\n// them here. Building the server in a factory, rather than at module load,\n// lets the tests drive a real Client against it in-process over an in-memory\n// transport (#4854), and holding the allow-list in this closure rather than in\n// a module global lets two instances coexist in one process.\nimport { McpServer } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/server\";\nimport type { Root } from \"@modelcontextprotocol/server\";\nimport fs from \"fs/promises\";\nimport { createReadStream } from \"fs\";\nimport path from \"path\";\nimport { pathToFileURL } from \"url\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { minimatch } from \"minimatch\";\nimport { getValidRootDirectories } from \"./roots-utils.js\";\nimport {\n  formatSize,\n  validatePath,\n  getFileStats,\n  readFileContent,\n  writeFileContent,\n  createDirectory,\n  moveFile,\n  searchFilesWithValidation,\n  applyFileEdits,\n  tailFile,\n  headFile,\n} from \"./lib.js\";\nimport { SERVER_VERSION } from \"./version.js\";\n\n// The JSON Schema dialect every advertised tool schema declares (#4841). The\n// SDK 1.x tools/list handler rendered zod schemas with zod's draft-07 target and\n// stamped \"$schema\": draft-07 on each one, which validators that accept only\n// 2020-12 (the dialect MCP assumes for tool schemas) reject outright. Setting\n// $schema in the root object's zod metadata overrode that stamp. SDK v2 stamps\n// 2020-12 itself, so the label is now the SDK's default as well. The schemas\n// below use nothing whose draft-07 and 2020-12 renderings differ (no tuples),\n// so the label is the only change; server-tools.test.ts fails if a draft-07-only\n// keyword ever appears under it.\nconst JSON_SCHEMA_DIALECT = \"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\";\n\n/** A tool's input or output shape as an object schema declaring 2020-12. */\nfunction jsonSchema2020<Shape extends z.ZodRawShape>(shape: Shape) {\n  return z.object(shape).meta({ $schema: JSON_SCHEMA_DIALECT });\n}\n\n// Schema definitions\nconst ReadTextFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n  tail: z\n    .number()\n    .optional()\n    .describe(\"If provided, returns only the last N lines of the file\"),\n  head: z\n    .number()\n    .optional()\n    .describe(\"If provided, returns only the first N lines of the file\"),\n});\n\nconst ReadMediaFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst ReadMultipleFilesArgsSchema = z.object({\n  paths: z\n    .array(z.string())\n    .min(1)\n    .describe(\n      \"Array of file paths to read. Each path must be a string pointing to a valid file within allowed directories.\",\n    ),\n});\n\nconst WriteFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n  content: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst EditOperation = z.object({\n  oldText: z.string().describe(\"Text to search for - must match exactly\"),\n  newText: z.string().describe(\"Text to replace with\"),\n});\n\nconst EditFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n  edits: z.array(EditOperation),\n  dryRun: z\n    .boolean()\n    .default(false)\n    .describe(\"Preview changes using git-style diff format\"),\n});\n\nconst CreateDirectoryArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst ListDirectoryArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst ListDirectoryWithSizesArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n  sortBy: z\n    .enum([\"name\", \"size\"])\n    .optional()\n    .default(\"name\")\n    .describe(\"Sort entries by name or size\"),\n});\n\nconst DirectoryTreeArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n  excludePatterns: z.array(z.string()).optional().default([]),\n});\n\nconst MoveFileArgsSchema = z.object({\n  source: z.string(),\n  destination: z.string(),\n});\n\nconst SearchFilesArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n  pattern: z.string(),\n  excludePatterns: z.array(z.string()).optional().default([]),\n});\n\nconst GetFileInfoArgsSchema = z.object({\n  path: z.string(),\n});\n\n// Reads a file as a stream of buffers, concatenates them, and then encodes\n// the result to a Base64 string. This is a memory-efficient way to handle\n// binary data from a stream before the final encoding.\nasync function readFileAsBase64Stream(filePath: string): Promise<string> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const stream = createReadStream(filePath);\n    const chunks: Buffer[] = [];\n    stream.on(\"data\", (chunk) => {\n      chunks.push(chunk as Buffer);\n    });\n    stream.on(\"end\", () => {\n      const finalBuffer = Buffer.concat(chunks);\n      resolve(finalBuffer.toString(\"base64\"));\n    });\n    stream.on(\"error\", (err) => reject(err));\n  });\n}\n\n/**\n * Why the server cannot operate when it has no allowed directories from either\n * the command line or the client's Roots (#4992).\n */\nexport const NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_ERROR =\n  \"Server cannot operate: No allowed directories available. Server was started without command-line directories and client does not support MCP roots protocol. Please either: 1) Start server with directory arguments, or 2) Use a client that supports MCP roots protocol and provides valid root directories.\";\n\n/**\n * Why the server cannot operate on a 2026-07-28 connection when it was started\n * with no allowed directories. That revision has no initialize handshake and\n * no server-to-client roots/list, so the client's Roots cannot supply them\n * (Roots is deprecated as of 2026-07-28); only the command line can (#4852).\n */\nexport const NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_MODERN_ERROR =\n  \"Server cannot operate: No allowed directories available. Server was started without command-line directories, and on protocol version 2026-07-28 the client cannot provide them through MCP Roots. Restart the server with the directories it may access as command-line arguments.\";\n\nexport interface ServerOptions {\n  /**\n   * The protocol era the server will serve (default `legacy`). On a\n   * 2025-era connection the client's Roots, when it supports them, replace\n   * the command-line directories after initialize. A 2026-07-28 connection\n   * has no initialize and no roots/list, so it is limited to the\n   * command-line directories.\n   */\n  era?: \"legacy\" | \"modern\";\n  /**\n   * Called after the server has closed the connection because it cannot\n   * operate: no directories were given and the client does not support\n   * Roots (#4992). The stdio entry point exits the process from here.\n   */\n  onCannotOperate?: (error: Error) => void;\n}\n\n/**\n * Build a filesystem server that may touch only `initialAllowedDirectories`\n * (already resolved and normalized by the caller), until the client's Roots\n * replace them on a 2025-era connection.\n */\nexport function createServer(\n  initialAllowedDirectories: string[],\n  options: ServerOptions = {},\n): McpServer {\n  let allowedDirectories = [...initialAllowedDirectories];\n  // True once the client's roots have replaced the command-line directories.\n  // From then on a roots update replaces them even with none, so a client that\n  // withdraws its roots withdraws the server's access too (#5094). Until then an\n  // update with no valid roots keeps the command-line directories.\n  let rootsInForce = false;\n  // Each roots refresh (the initial load and every roots/list_changed) takes\n  // the next generation before it asks for roots. The SDK runs notification\n  // handlers without waiting for earlier ones, so answers can come back out of\n  // order; one is applied only if no newer refresh has started since, so a\n  // stale answer cannot undo a newer update, such as a revocation (#5097).\n  let rootsGeneration = 0;\n\n  const server = new McpServer({\n    name: \"secure-filesystem-server\",\n    version: SERVER_VERSION,\n  });\n\n  // The barrier every tool call waits on, so a call that arrives while roots are\n  // being fetched is checked against the roots that come back rather than the\n  // ones being replaced: the command-line directories while the initial\n  // roots/list is outstanding (#3204), or a root the client has just withdrawn\n  // while a roots/list_changed refresh is pending (#5101). Each refresh (the\n  // initial load included) installs a new barrier before it sends roots/list,\n  // and settles it when it finishes, successfully or not. Installing one also\n  // settles the barrier it replaces, so calls waiting on an older refresh wake\n  // at once and move on to the newer one instead of waiting for the older\n  // one's answer (#5097, #5101).\n  let barrier = { settled: Promise.resolve(), settle: () => {} };\n  function installBarrier(): () => void {\n    let settle!: () => void;\n    const settled = new Promise<void>((resolve) => (settle = resolve));\n    const replaced = barrier;\n    barrier = { settled, settle };\n    replaced.settle();\n    return settle;\n  }\n  const registerTool = server.registerTool.bind(server);\n  server.registerTool = ((\n    name: string,\n    config: Parameters<typeof registerTool>[1],\n    handler: (...args: unknown[]) => unknown,\n  ) =>\n    registerTool(name, config, (async (...args: unknown[]) => {\n      let awaited: typeof barrier;\n      do {\n        awaited = barrier;\n        await awaited.settled;\n      } while (awaited !== barrier);\n      // A 2025-era session with no directories closes at initialize (#4992).\n      // A 2026-07-28 one has no initialize to close at, and its client cannot\n      // add directories, so every call says why it cannot run.\n      if (options.era === \"modern\" && allowedDirectories.length === 0) {\n        return {\n          content: [\n            { type: \"text\", text: NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_MODERN_ERROR },\n          ],\n          isError: true,\n        };\n      }\n      return handler(...args);\n    }) as never)) as typeof server.registerTool;\n\n  // Tool registrations\n\n  // read_file (deprecated) and read_text_file\n  const readTextFileHandler = async (\n    args: z.infer<typeof ReadTextFileArgsSchema>,\n  ) => {\n    const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n\n    if (args.head && args.tail) {\n      throw new Error(\n        \"Cannot specify both head and tail parameters simultaneously\",\n      );\n    }\n\n    let content: string;\n    if (args.tail) {\n      content = await tailFile(validPath, args.tail);\n    } else if (args.head) {\n      content = await headFile(validPath, args.head);\n    } else {\n      content = await readFileContent(validPath);\n    }\n\n    return {\n      content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: content }],\n      structuredContent: { content },\n    };\n  };\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"read_file\",\n    {\n      title: \"Read File (Deprecated)\",\n      description:\n        \"Read the complete contents of a file as text. DEPRECATED: Use read_text_file instead.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ReadTextFileArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    readTextFileHandler,\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"read_text_file\",\n    {\n      title: \"Read Text File\",\n      description:\n        \"Read the complete contents of a file from the file system as text. \" +\n        \"Handles various text encodings and provides detailed error messages \" +\n        \"if the file cannot be read. Use this tool when you need to examine \" +\n        \"the contents of a single file. Use the 'head' parameter to read only \" +\n        \"the first N lines of a file, or the 'tail' parameter to read only \" +\n        \"the last N lines of a file. Operates on the file as text regardless of extension. \" +\n        \"Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020({\n        path: z.string(),\n        tail: z\n          .number()\n          .optional()\n          .describe(\"If provided, returns only the last N lines of the file\"),\n        head: z\n          .number()\n          .optional()\n          .describe(\"If provided, returns only the first N lines of the file\"),\n      }),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    readTextFileHandler,\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"read_media_file\",\n    {\n      title: \"Read Media File\",\n      description:\n        \"Read a file and return it as a base64-encoded content block with its MIME type. \" +\n        \"Image and audio files are returned as image/audio content; any other file type is \" +\n        \"returned as an embedded resource. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ReadMediaFileArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({\n        content: z.array(\n          z.union([\n            z.object({\n              type: z.enum([\"image\", \"audio\"]),\n              data: z.string(),\n              mimeType: z.string(),\n            }),\n            z.object({\n              type: z.literal(\"resource\"),\n              resource: z.object({\n                uri: z.string(),\n                // Optional, matching the SDK's BlobResourceContents shape (the handler always sets it).\n                mimeType: z.string().optional(),\n                blob: z.string(),\n              }),\n            }),\n          ]),\n        ),\n      }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof ReadMediaFileArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      const extension = path.extname(validPath).toLowerCase();\n      const mimeTypes: Record<string, string> = {\n        \".png\": \"image/png\",\n        \".jpg\": \"image/jpeg\",\n        \".jpeg\": \"image/jpeg\",\n        \".gif\": \"image/gif\",\n        \".webp\": \"image/webp\",\n        \".bmp\": \"image/bmp\",\n        \".svg\": \"image/svg+xml\",\n        \".mp3\": \"audio/mpeg\",\n        \".wav\": \"audio/wav\",\n        \".ogg\": \"audio/ogg\",\n        \".flac\": \"audio/flac\",\n      };\n      const mimeType = mimeTypes[extension] || \"application/octet-stream\";\n      const data = await readFileAsBase64Stream(validPath);\n\n      // Map the MIME type to a valid MCP content block. The spec only allows\n      // text, image, audio, resource_link, and resource — so non-image/audio\n      // binaries are returned as an embedded resource (NOT type:\"blob\", which the\n      // SDK content-block union rejects on schema validation).\n      const contentItem = mimeType.startsWith(\"image/\")\n        ? { type: \"image\" as const, data, mimeType }\n        : mimeType.startsWith(\"audio/\")\n          ? { type: \"audio\" as const, data, mimeType }\n          : {\n              type: \"resource\" as const,\n              resource: {\n                uri: pathToFileURL(validPath).href,\n                mimeType,\n                blob: data,\n              },\n            };\n      return {\n        content: [contentItem],\n        structuredContent: { content: [contentItem] },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"read_multiple_files\",\n    {\n      title: \"Read Multiple Files\",\n      description:\n        \"Read the contents of multiple files simultaneously. This is more \" +\n        \"efficient than reading files one by one when you need to analyze \" +\n        \"or compare multiple files. Each file's content is returned with its \" +\n        \"path as a reference. Failed reads for individual files won't stop \" +\n        \"the entire operation. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ReadMultipleFilesArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof ReadMultipleFilesArgsSchema>) => {\n      const results = await Promise.all(\n        args.paths.map(async (filePath: string) => {\n          try {\n            const validPath = await validatePath(filePath, allowedDirectories);\n            const content = await readFileContent(validPath);\n            return `${filePath}:\\n${content}\\n`;\n          } catch (error) {\n            const errorMessage =\n              error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);\n            return `${filePath}: Error - ${errorMessage}`;\n          }\n        }),\n      );\n      const text = results.join(\"\\n---\\n\");\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"write_file\",\n    {\n      title: \"Write File\",\n      description:\n        \"Create a new file or completely overwrite an existing file with new content. \" +\n        \"Use with caution as it will overwrite existing files without warning. \" +\n        \"Handles text content with proper encoding. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(WriteFileArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: {\n        readOnlyHint: false,\n        idempotentHint: true,\n        destructiveHint: true,\n        openWorldHint: false,\n      },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof WriteFileArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      await writeFileContent(validPath, args.content);\n      const text = `Successfully wrote to ${args.path}`;\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"edit_file\",\n    {\n      title: \"Edit File\",\n      description:\n        \"Make line-based edits to a text file. Each edit replaces exact line sequences \" +\n        \"with new content. Returns a git-style diff showing the changes made. \" +\n        \"Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(EditFileArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: {\n        readOnlyHint: false,\n        idempotentHint: false,\n        destructiveHint: true,\n        openWorldHint: false,\n      },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof EditFileArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      const result = await applyFileEdits(validPath, args.edits, args.dryRun);\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: result }],\n        structuredContent: { content: result },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"create_directory\",\n    {\n      title: \"Create Directory\",\n      description:\n        \"Create a new directory or ensure a directory exists. Can create multiple \" +\n        \"nested directories in one operation. If the directory already exists, \" +\n        \"this operation will succeed silently. Perfect for setting up directory \" +\n        \"structures for projects or ensuring required paths exist. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(CreateDirectoryArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: {\n        readOnlyHint: false,\n        idempotentHint: true,\n        destructiveHint: false,\n        openWorldHint: false,\n      },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof CreateDirectoryArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      await createDirectory(validPath);\n      const text = `Successfully created directory ${args.path}`;\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"list_directory\",\n    {\n      title: \"List Directory\",\n      description:\n        \"Get a detailed listing of all files and directories in a specified path. \" +\n        \"Results clearly distinguish between files and directories with [FILE] and [DIR] \" +\n        \"prefixes. This tool is essential for understanding directory structure and \" +\n        \"finding specific files within a directory. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ListDirectoryArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof ListDirectoryArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      const entries = await fs.readdir(validPath, { withFileTypes: true });\n      const formatted = entries\n        .map(\n          (entry) =>\n            `${entry.isDirectory() ? \"[DIR]\" : \"[FILE]\"} ${entry.name}`,\n        )\n        .join(\"\\n\");\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text: formatted }],\n        structuredContent: { content: formatted },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"list_directory_with_sizes\",\n    {\n      title: \"List Directory with Sizes\",\n      description:\n        \"Get a detailed listing of all files and directories in a specified path, including sizes. \" +\n        \"Results clearly distinguish between files and directories with [FILE] and [DIR] \" +\n        \"prefixes. This tool is useful for understanding directory structure and \" +\n        \"finding specific files within a directory. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(ListDirectoryWithSizesArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof ListDirectoryWithSizesArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      const entries = await fs.readdir(validPath, { withFileTypes: true });\n\n      // Get detailed information for each entry\n      const detailedEntries = await Promise.all(\n        entries.map(async (entry) => {\n          const entryPath = path.join(validPath, entry.name);\n          try {\n            // fs.stat follows symlinks, so validate each entry first: a\n            // symlink whose target is outside the allowed directories is\n            // listed with no size rather than the target's.\n            const validEntryPath = await validatePath(\n              entryPath,\n              allowedDirectories,\n            );\n            const stats = await fs.stat(validEntryPath);\n            return {\n              name: entry.name,\n              isDirectory: entry.isDirectory(),\n              size: stats.size,\n              mtime: stats.mtime,\n            };\n          } catch {\n            return {\n              name: entry.name,\n              isDirectory: entry.isDirectory(),\n              size: 0,\n              mtime: new Date(0),\n            };\n          }\n        }),\n      );\n\n      // Sort entries based on sortBy parameter\n      const sortedEntries = [...detailedEntries].sort((a, b) => {\n        if (args.sortBy === \"size\") {\n          return b.size - a.size; // Descending by size\n        }\n        // Default sort by name\n        return a.name.localeCompare(b.name);\n      });\n\n      // Format the output\n      const formattedEntries = sortedEntries.map(\n        (entry) =>\n          `${entry.isDirectory ? \"[DIR]\" : \"[FILE]\"} ${entry.name.padEnd(30)} ${\n            entry.isDirectory ? \"\" : formatSize(entry.size).padStart(10)\n          }`,\n      );\n\n      // Add summary\n      const totalFiles = detailedEntries.filter((e) => !e.isDirectory).length;\n      const totalDirs = detailedEntries.filter((e) => e.isDirectory).length;\n      const totalSize = detailedEntries.reduce(\n        (sum, entry) => sum + (entry.isDirectory ? 0 : entry.size),\n        0,\n      );\n\n      const summary = [\n        \"\",\n        `Total: ${totalFiles} files, ${totalDirs} directories`,\n        `Combined size: ${formatSize(totalSize)}`,\n      ];\n\n      const text = [...formattedEntries, ...summary].join(\"\\n\");\n      const contentBlock = { type: \"text\" as const, text };\n      return {\n        content: [contentBlock],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"directory_tree\",\n    {\n      title: \"Directory Tree\",\n      description:\n        \"Get a recursive tree view of files and directories as a JSON structure. \" +\n        \"Each entry includes 'name', 'type' (file/directory), and 'children' for directories. \" +\n        \"Files have no children array, while directories always have a children array (which may be empty). \" +\n        \"The output is formatted with 2-space indentation for readability. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(DirectoryTreeArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof DirectoryTreeArgsSchema>) => {\n      interface TreeEntry {\n        name: string;\n        type: \"file\" | \"directory\";\n        children?: TreeEntry[];\n      }\n      const rootPath = args.path;\n\n      async function buildTree(\n        currentPath: string,\n        excludePatterns: string[] = [],\n      ): Promise<TreeEntry[]> {\n        const validPath = await validatePath(currentPath, allowedDirectories);\n        const entries = await fs.readdir(validPath, { withFileTypes: true });\n        const result: TreeEntry[] = [];\n\n        for (const entry of entries) {\n          const relativePath = path.relative(\n            rootPath,\n            path.join(currentPath, entry.name),\n          );\n          const shouldExclude = excludePatterns.some((pattern) => {\n            if (pattern.includes(\"*\")) {\n              return minimatch(relativePath, pattern, { dot: true });\n            }\n            // For files: match exact name or as part of path\n            // For directories: match as directory path\n            return (\n              minimatch(relativePath, pattern, { dot: true }) ||\n              minimatch(relativePath, `**/${pattern}`, { dot: true }) ||\n              minimatch(relativePath, `**/${pattern}/**`, { dot: true })\n            );\n          });\n          if (shouldExclude) continue;\n\n          const entryData: TreeEntry = {\n            name: entry.name,\n            type: entry.isDirectory() ? \"directory\" : \"file\",\n          };\n\n          if (entry.isDirectory()) {\n            const subPath = path.join(currentPath, entry.name);\n            entryData.children = await buildTree(subPath, excludePatterns);\n          }\n\n          result.push(entryData);\n        }\n\n        return result;\n      }\n\n      const treeData = await buildTree(rootPath, args.excludePatterns);\n      const text = JSON.stringify(treeData, null, 2);\n      const contentBlock = { type: \"text\" as const, text };\n      return {\n        content: [contentBlock],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"move_file\",\n    {\n      title: \"Move File\",\n      description:\n        \"Move or rename files and directories. Can move files between directories \" +\n        \"and rename them in a single operation. If the destination exists, the \" +\n        \"operation will fail. Works across different directories and can be used \" +\n        \"for simple renaming within the same directory. Both source and destination must be within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(MoveFileArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: {\n        readOnlyHint: false,\n        idempotentHint: false,\n        destructiveHint: true,\n        openWorldHint: false,\n      },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof MoveFileArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validSourcePath = await validatePath(\n        args.source,\n        allowedDirectories,\n      );\n      const validDestPath = await validatePath(\n        args.destination,\n        allowedDirectories,\n      );\n      await moveFile(validSourcePath, validDestPath);\n      const text = `Successfully moved ${args.source} to ${args.destination}`;\n      const contentBlock = { type: \"text\" as const, text };\n      return {\n        content: [contentBlock],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"search_files\",\n    {\n      title: \"Search Files\",\n      description:\n        \"Recursively search for files and directories matching a pattern. \" +\n        \"The patterns should be glob-style patterns that match paths relative to the working directory. \" +\n        \"Use pattern like '*.ext' to match files in current directory, and '**/*.ext' to match files in all subdirectories. \" +\n        \"Returns full paths to all matching items. Great for finding files when you don't know their exact location. \" +\n        \"Only searches within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(SearchFilesArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof SearchFilesArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      const results = await searchFilesWithValidation(\n        validPath,\n        args.pattern,\n        allowedDirectories,\n        { excludePatterns: args.excludePatterns },\n      );\n      const text = results.length > 0 ? results.join(\"\\n\") : \"No matches found\";\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"get_file_info\",\n    {\n      title: \"Get File Info\",\n      description:\n        \"Retrieve detailed metadata about a file or directory. Returns comprehensive \" +\n        \"information including size, creation time, last modified time, permissions, \" +\n        \"and type. This tool is perfect for understanding file characteristics \" +\n        \"without reading the actual content. Only works within allowed directories.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020(GetFileInfoArgsSchema.shape),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async (args: z.infer<typeof GetFileInfoArgsSchema>) => {\n      const validPath = await validatePath(args.path, allowedDirectories);\n      const info = await getFileStats(validPath);\n      const text = Object.entries(info)\n        .map(([key, value]) => `${key}: ${value}`)\n        .join(\"\\n\");\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  server.registerTool(\n    \"list_allowed_directories\",\n    {\n      title: \"List Allowed Directories\",\n      description:\n        \"Returns the list of directories that this server is allowed to access. \" +\n        \"Subdirectories within these allowed directories are also accessible. \" +\n        \"Use this to understand which directories and their nested paths are available \" +\n        \"before trying to access files.\",\n      inputSchema: jsonSchema2020({}),\n      outputSchema: jsonSchema2020({ content: z.string() }),\n      annotations: { readOnlyHint: true, openWorldHint: false },\n    },\n    async () => {\n      const text = `Allowed directories:\\n${allowedDirectories.join(\"\\n\")}`;\n      return {\n        content: [{ type: \"text\" as const, text }],\n        structuredContent: { content: text },\n      };\n    },\n  );\n\n  // Updates allowed directories based on MCP client roots, unless a newer\n  // refresh started after this one's `generation` was taken (#5097).\n  async function updateAllowedDirectoriesFromRoots(\n    requestedRoots: Root[],\n    generation: number,\n  ) {\n    const validatedRootDirs = await getValidRootDirectories(requestedRoots);\n    if (generation !== rootsGeneration) {\n      console.error(\n        \"Discarded a stale roots/list answer: a newer roots update has started\",\n      );\n      return;\n    }\n    if (validatedRootDirs.length > 0) {\n      allowedDirectories = [...validatedRootDirs];\n      rootsInForce = true;\n      console.error(\n        `Updated allowed directories from MCP roots: ${validatedRootDirs.length} valid directories`,\n      );\n    } else if (rootsInForce) {\n      allowedDirectories = [];\n      console.error(\n        \"No valid root directories provided by client; access revoked until it exposes a root again\",\n      );\n    } else {\n      console.error(\"No valid root directories provided by client\");\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Handles dynamic roots updates during runtime, when client sends \"roots/list_changed\" notification, server fetches the updated roots and replaces all allowed directories with the new roots.\n  server.server.setNotificationHandler(\n    \"notifications/roots/list_changed\",\n    async () => {\n      const generation = ++rootsGeneration;\n      const settle = installBarrier();\n      try {\n        // Request the updated roots list from the client\n        const response = await server.server.listRoots();\n        /* v8 ignore else -- the SDK validates the roots/list result against ListRootsResultSchema, which requires roots, so the implicit else cannot run */\n        if (response && \"roots\" in response) {\n          await updateAllowedDirectoriesFromRoots(response.roots, generation);\n        }\n      } catch (error) {\n        console.error(\n          \"Failed to request roots from client:\",\n          error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n        );\n      } finally {\n        settle();\n      }\n    },\n  );\n\n  // Handles post-initialization setup, specifically checking for and fetching\n  // MCP roots. Only a 2025-era connection initializes, so this never runs on\n  // 2026-07-28, where Roots is unavailable (and deprecated). The initial load claims its generation and installs its barrier\n  // here, synchronously, before any roots/list_changed handled afterwards can\n  // claim a newer one.\n  server.server.oninitialized = () => {\n    const generation = ++rootsGeneration;\n    const settle = installBarrier();\n    // Not awaited: oninitialized is synchronous. Tool calls wait on the\n    // barrier, which settles when initialize() finishes; it never rejects.\n    void initialize(generation).finally(settle);\n  };\n\n  async function initialize(generation: number): Promise<void> {\n    const clientCapabilities = server.server.getClientCapabilities();\n\n    if (clientCapabilities?.roots) {\n      try {\n        const response = await server.server.listRoots();\n        /* v8 ignore else -- the SDK validates the roots/list result against ListRootsResultSchema, which requires roots, so the else cannot run */\n        if (response && \"roots\" in response) {\n          await updateAllowedDirectoriesFromRoots(response.roots, generation);\n        } else {\n          console.error(\n            \"Client returned no roots set, keeping current settings\",\n          );\n        }\n      } catch (error) {\n        console.error(\n          \"Failed to request initial roots from client:\",\n          error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),\n        );\n      }\n    } else {\n      if (allowedDirectories.length > 0) {\n        console.error(\n          \"Client does not support MCP Roots, using allowed directories set from server args:\",\n          allowedDirectories,\n        );\n      } else {\n        // Nothing could ever be allowed in this session, so fail visibly\n        // rather than serve every call with \"Access denied\" (#4992): log the\n        // reason and close the connection. Throwing here would only reach the\n        // SDK's onerror, which the client never sees.\n        const error = new Error(NO_ALLOWED_DIRECTORIES_ERROR);\n        console.error(`Error: ${error.message}`);\n        await server.close();\n        options.onCannotOperate?.(error);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return server;\n}\n","numLines":928,"startLine":1,"totalLines":928}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DBuqZkqrXAYCfW7cMCrEqZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
